Since you need **three** themes, the `ResourceDictionary` swap approach is the way to go (`AppThemeBinding` only supports two). Here's the full setup:

## 1. Define Theme Dictionaries

Create three files with **matching keys** under a `Themes/` folder:

**LightTheme.xaml**
```xml
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
                    x:Class="MyApp.Themes.LightTheme">
    <Color x:Key="PageBackgroundColor">White</Color>
    <Color x:Key="PrimaryTextColor">#333333</Color>
    <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#2196F3</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
```

**DarkTheme.xaml**
```xml
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
                    x:Class="MyApp.Themes.DarkTheme">
    <Color x:Key="PageBackgroundColor">#1E1E1E</Color>
    <Color x:Key="PrimaryTextColor">#F0F0F0</Color>
    <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#BB86FC</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
```

**HighContrastTheme.xaml**
```xml
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
                    x:Class="MyApp.Themes.HighContrastTheme">
    <Color x:Key="PageBackgroundColor">Black</Color>
    <Color x:Key="PrimaryTextColor">White</Color>
    <Color x:Key="AccentColor">Yellow</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
```

Each needs a code-behind:
```csharp
namespace MyApp.Themes;
public partial class LightTheme : ResourceDictionary
{
    public LightTheme() => InitializeComponent();
}
// Same pattern for DarkTheme and HighContrastTheme
```

## 2. Use `DynamicResource` Everywhere

```xml
<ContentPage BackgroundColor="{DynamicResource PageBackgroundColor}">
    <Label Text="Hello" TextColor="{DynamicResource PrimaryTextColor}" />
    <Button Text="Go" BackgroundColor="{DynamicResource AccentColor}" />
</ContentPage>
```

## 3. Theme Switching Service

```csharp
public static class ThemeService
{
    public static void ApplyTheme(string themeName)
    {
        var mergedDictionaries = Application.Current!.Resources.MergedDictionaries;
        mergedDictionaries.Clear();
        mergedDictionaries.Add(themeName switch
        {
            "Dark"         => new DarkTheme(),
            "HighContrast" => new HighContrastTheme(),
            _              => new LightTheme()
        });

        Preferences.Set("AppTheme", themeName);
    }

    public static void LoadSavedTheme()
    {
        var saved = Preferences.Get("AppTheme", "Light");
        ApplyTheme(saved);
    }
}
```

## 4. Apply on Startup (App.xaml.cs)

```csharp
public App()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    ThemeService.LoadSavedTheme();
}
```

## 5. Settings Page

```csharp
void OnThemeSelected(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    string choice = themePicker.SelectedItem as string; // "Light", "Dark", "HighContrast"
    ThemeService.ApplyTheme(choice);
}
```

## Key Pitfalls to Avoid

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| Using `StaticResource` | Must use `DynamicResource` or values freeze |
| Missing key in one dictionary | Every `x:Key` must exist in all three dictionaries |
| Android activity restart on theme change | Add `ConfigChanges.UiMode` to `MainActivity` |

The theme applies immediately because `DynamicResource` re-evaluates when the merged dictionary changes — no page reload needed.
